preguntas.respuestaEn:
"Both describe the shape of an object, and in most cases they are interchangeable. `interface` is extended with `extends` and can be reopened to add fields. `type` is more flexible: it can be a union, a tuple or a name for a primitive type. My rule is `interface` for objects and props, and `type` when I need unions; what matters is being consistent across the project."

```ts
interface Usuario {
  id: number;
  nombre: string;
}
interface Admin extends Usuario {
  permisos: string[];
}

type Estado = "cargando" | "error" | "listo"; // esto solo se puede con type
type Punto = [number, number];
```

"`any` turns type checking off: I can do anything with that value and TypeScript says nothing, so I lose the reason for using it. `unknown` also accepts any value, but it will not let me use it until I check what it is. So for data I do not control, like an API response or a `JSON.parse` result, I use `unknown` and validate before using it."

```ts
function longitud(valor: unknown): number {
  // valor.length; // error: todavía no sé qué es
  if (typeof valor === "string") return valor.length; // aquí ya es string
  if (Array.isArray(valor)) return valor.length;
  return 0;
}
```

"A union says a value can be one type or another, for example `string | number`. Since TypeScript does not know which one it is, it only lets me use what they have in common. Narrowing means checking the type with an `if`, for example with `typeof`, and inside that block TypeScript knows exactly what it is and lets me use its methods."

```ts
function formatearId(id: string | number): string {
  if (typeof id === "number") {
    return id.toFixed(0); // aquí id es number
  }
  return id.toUpperCase(); // aquí id es string
}
```

"They are like parameters, but for types. They let me write a function or a type that works with any type without losing information. For example, a function that returns the first item of an array: if I pass numbers, TypeScript knows it returns a number; if I pass users, it knows it returns a user. With `any` I would lose that information."

```ts
function primero<T>(lista: T[]): T | undefined {
  return lista[0];
}
const n = primero([1, 2, 3]);           // number | undefined
const u = primero([{ nombre: "Ana" }]); // { nombre: string } | undefined
```

"They are types built into TypeScript that transform other types without rewriting them. `Partial` makes every property optional, which is handy for an edit form. `Pick` and `Omit` keep or remove fields, for example a user without the password. `Record` is for dictionary-like objects and `Readonly` prevents changes."

```ts
interface Producto { id: number; nombre: string; precio: number; stock: number }

type CambiosProducto = Partial<Producto>;          // todo opcional
type ProductoNuevo = Omit<Producto, "id">;         // el id lo pone el servidor
type Resumen = Pick<Producto, "nombre" | "precio">;
const stockPorTienda: Record<string, number> = { lima: 10, cusco: 4 };
```

"It is a union of objects that share a property with a different literal value in each one, for example `estado`. By checking that property, TypeScript knows which object it is and what other fields it has. I use it to model request states: if the state is `error` I have the message, if it is `listo` I have the data, and I cannot read data that does not exist yet."

```ts
type Peticion =
  | { estado: "cargando" }
  | { estado: "error"; mensaje: string }
  | { estado: "listo"; datos: string[] };

function mostrar(p: Peticion): string {
  switch (p.estado) {
    case "cargando": return "Cargando...";
    case "error": return p.mensaje;          // aquí existe mensaje
    case "listo": return p.datos.join(", "); // aquí existe datos
  }
}
```

"I define a type with the props and put it on the component's parameter, usually destructuring. Optional props get a question mark and a default value. For children I use `React.ReactNode`, and for events I use React's types, like the one for an input's `onChange`. I also type state when it cannot be inferred, like a `useState` that starts as `null`."

```tsx
type BotonProps = {
  texto: string;
  variante?: "primario" | "secundario";
  onClick: () => void;
};

function Boton({ texto, variante = "primario", onClick }: BotonProps) {
  return <button className={variante} onClick={onClick}>{texto}</button>;
}

const [usuario, setUsuario] = useState<Usuario | null>(null);
```

"They are erased. TypeScript checks the types and then emits plain JavaScript with no annotations, so at runtime they do not exist. That means if an API returns something different from what I declared, TypeScript does not save me: the error still happens. That is why I validate external data at runtime, by hand or with a library like Zod."

```ts
// Esto compila, pero es una promesa que yo hago, no una garantía:
const usuario = (await respuesta.json()) as Usuario;

// En ejecución hay que comprobarlo de verdad:
if (typeof usuario.nombre !== "string") throw new Error("Respuesta inválida");
```

"Gradually, not all at once. First I add TypeScript and a `tsconfig` with `allowJs`, so `.js` and `.ts` files can live together. Then I convert file by file, starting with the ones that do not depend on others, like utilities and the types for API data. At first I use a looser configuration and turn on `strict` once the base is typed. That way the project keeps working at every step."

```json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "allowJs": true,
    "checkJs": false,
    "strict": false,
    "noEmit": true
  }
}
```
